---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getSeoMetadata } from '../utils/seo';

const seo = getSeoMetadata('/how-to-adjust-ski-bindings/', 'en');

const tocItems = [
  { id: 'anatomy', title: '1. Three Binding Mechanisms' },
  { id: 'forward-pressure', title: '2. Forward Pressure Trap' },
  { id: 'brand-indicators', title: '3. Brand Pressure Indicators' },
  { id: 'shop-protocol', title: '4. Professional Shop Protocol' },
  { id: 'diy-mistakes', title: '5. 4 Fatal DIY Mistakes' },
  { id: 'tech-card', title: '6. Print Technician Card' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={seo.title}
  description={seo.description}
  canonicalUrl={seo.canonicalUrl}
  ogType="article"
>
  <article class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 md:py-20 text-ink">
    {/* Breadcrumbs */}
    <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-mono text-mute mb-8" aria-label="Breadcrumbs">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors">Home</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <a href="/methodology/" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors">Safety Guides</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span class="text-ink">Binding Adjustment Guide</span>
    </nav>

    {/* Header */}
    <header class="space-y-4 mb-8">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-parchment border border-hairline text-ink text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider">
        <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-amber-500"></span>
        <span>Safety & Technical Guide · ISO 11088:2023</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">
        How Ski Bindings Work: Screws & Shop Calibration
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-mute leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
        Understand how ski binding release springs, indicator windows, and forward pressure mechanisms operate, and why professional workshop torque testing is critical to ski safety.
      </p>

      {/* Meta Bar */}
      <div class="pt-4 border-t border-hairline flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-xs text-mute font-mono">
        <span>Standard: ISO 11088:2023, ASTM F939 & ASTM F1063</span>
        <span>•</span>
        <span>Audited: September 2026</span>
        <span>•</span>
        <span class="text-amber-500 font-medium">NEEDS-HUMAN: Shop Technician Review</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    {/* Mobile Jump Menu (Horizontal scroll pills) */}
    <div class="lg:hidden mb-8 pb-2 overflow-x-auto flex gap-2 no-scrollbar border-b border-hairline pt-2">
      {tocItems.map(item => (
        <a
          href={`#${item.id}`}
          class="shrink-0 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-parchment border border-hairline text-xs font-mono text-mute hover:text-primary hover:border-primary/40 transition-colors"
        >
          {item.title}
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>

    {/* Two-Column Grid: Main Content + Sticky TOC Sidebar */}
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-8 items-start">
      {/* Main Content Column */}
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-12">
        {/* Answer-First Summary Block */}
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20 space-y-3 shadow-sm text-ink">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-bold font-mono text-amber-600 uppercase tracking-wider">
            <svg class="w-4 h-4 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z" />
            </svg>
            Critical Safety Notice: Never Guess or DIY Adjust
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
            While visual indicator scales on toe and heel pieces can be turned with a screwdriver, <strong>turning the screw alone does NOT correctly adjust your ski bindings</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-mute">
            A ski binding requires proper forward pressure preload, toe-height AFD clearance, and mechanical release torque verification on a calibrated test bench (ASTM F1063 / ISO 11088). Incorrect DIY adjustment is a leading cause of severe knee tears and tibial fractures.
          </p>
        </div>

        {/* 1. Anatomy of a Ski Binding */}
        <section id="anatomy" class="space-y-4 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">
            1. The Three Mechanisms of an Alpine Ski Binding
          </h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-mute leading-relaxed">
            Every alpine ski binding contains three independent mechanical subsystems that work together to balance skier retention and injury release.
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-canvas border border-hairline space-y-2">
              <div class="text-primary font-bold text-sm">1. Toe Release Springs</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-mute leading-relaxed">
                Located in the front housing. Controls lateral release when twisting force exceeds the safe threshold, protecting the tibia from torsional spiral fracture.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-canvas border border-hairline space-y-2">
              <div class="text-primary font-bold text-sm">2. Heel Upward Springs</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-mute leading-relaxed">
                Located in the rear housing. Compresses vertically to release upward when pitching forward, protecting the knee and Achilles tendon from hyperflexion.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-canvas border border-hairline space-y-2">
              <div class="text-primary font-bold text-sm">3. Forward Pressure</div>
              <p class="text-xs text-mute leading-relaxed">
                The rear track spring that keeps the heel piece pressing firmly against the boot heel. This ensures the binding maintains constant torque while the ski flexes during turns.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* 2. The Hidden Factor: Forward Pressure */}
        <section id="forward-pressure" class="space-y-4 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">
            2. Why Forward Pressure is the #1 DIY Trap
          </h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-mute leading-relaxed">
            Most skiers believe that matching the number in the toe and heel indicator windows to their DIN chart is all that is required. <strong>This is dangerously untrue.</strong>
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-mute leading-relaxed">
            The forward pressure track absorbs ski decambering. When a ski flexes deeply into a turn or a bump trough, the physical distance between the toe and heel pieces shortens.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-mute leading-relaxed">
            If forward pressure is set incorrectly:
          </p>
          <ul class="text-xs text-mute space-y-2 font-mono list-disc pl-5 leading-relaxed">
            <li><strong>Too loose (insufficient pressure):</strong> The boot ejects prematurely during turns or high-speed compressions (pre-release), causing high-speed crashes.</li>
            <li><strong>Too tight (excessive pressure):</strong> The heel binds against the boot lug, massively increasing release torque. In a twisting fall, the binding locks up, causing severe ACL/MCL ligament ruptures.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        {/* 3. Brand Specific Mechanisms */}
        <section id="brand-indicators" class="space-y-4 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">
            3. Brand-Specific Forward Pressure Indicators
          </h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-mute leading-relaxed">
            Different binding manufacturers employ distinct mechanical indicators to verify forward pressure when the ski boot is clicked into the binding.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-mute leading-relaxed">
            Always inspect these indicators with the skier's actual boot clicked completely into the binding:
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas shadow-sm">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs font-mono">
              <thead class="bg-parchment border-b border-hairline text-mute uppercase tracking-wider">
                <tr>
                  <th class="p-3">Manufacturer & Model</th>
                  <th class="p-3">Forward Pressure Mechanism</th>
                  <th class="p-3">Correct Verification Indicator</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline">
                <tr class="hover:bg-parchment/50">
                  <td class="p-3 font-bold text-ink">Marker (Griffon, Squire, Jester)</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Adjustment screw at rear base of heel track.</td>
                  <td class="p-3 text-mute">Screw head must sit completely flush with the surrounding plastic housing when boot is engaged.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="hover:bg-parchment/50">
                  <td class="p-3 font-bold text-ink">Look Pivot / Rossignol FKS</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Turntable heel arm screw arms.</td>
                  <td class="p-3 text-mute">Indicator white marks on the arms must align within the scribe marks on the turntable base.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="hover:bg-parchment/50">
                  <td class="p-3 font-bold text-ink">Salomon / Atomic (Warden, Strive)</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Worm screw on rear heel track.</td>
                  <td class="p-3 text-mute">Metal indicator tab must rest inside the marked scribe box on the heel track.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="hover:bg-parchment/50">
                  <td class="p-3 font-bold text-ink">Tyrolia / Head / Fischer (Attack)</td>
                  <td class="p-3">Track worm screw at the heel base.</td>
                  <td class="p-3 text-mute">Engaged metal indicator track notches align with the indicator casing mark.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* 4. What a Qualified Ski Shop Technician Does */}
        <section id="shop-protocol" class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-canvas border border-hairline space-y-4 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink">
            4. What a Qualified Ski Shop Technician Does
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-3 text-xs text-mute leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              When you bring your boots and skis to a certified ski shop, the technician performs a standardized 5-step protocol under ISO 11088 and ASTM F1063:
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-mono">
              <li><strong>Visual & Physical Inspection:</strong> Inspects boot soles for excessive wear, gouges, or beveling at toe/heel lugs, and verifies AFD condition.</li>
              <li><strong>BSL & Forward Pressure Calibration:</strong> Mounts boot into binding and accurately aligns forward pressure to manufacturer specifications.</li>
              <li><strong>Toe Height / AFD Clearance:</strong> Uses a calibrated feeler gauge to ensure exact specified clearance between boot sole and AFD.</li>
              <li><strong>Calibrated Mechanical Release Test:</strong> Places the ski-binding-boot assembly into a computerized torque test bench (e.g., Montana, Vermont Safety, Wintersteiger) to mechanically measure actual release torque in N·m.</li>
              <li><strong>Tolerance Documentation:</strong> Compares measured torque against ISO 11088 inspection window tolerances and delivers an official printed inspection report.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* 5. What NOT to Do */}
        <section id="diy-mistakes" class="space-y-4 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">
            5. The 4 Fatal DIY Ski Binding Mistakes
          </h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs">
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-parchment border border-hairline space-y-2">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm text-red-500">1. Using Stripped Hand Tools</h3>
              <p class="text-mute leading-relaxed">Ski binding screws use Pozidriv #3 bits, not standard Phillips #2. Using the wrong screwdriver strips the screw heads, destroying the binding housing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-parchment border border-hairline space-y-2">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm text-red-500">2. Mismatched Toe and Heel Values</h3>
              <p class="text-mute leading-relaxed">Except in specific manufacturer race protocols, toe and heel indicator scales must be set to the identical DIN value calculated from your skier profile.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-parchment border border-hairline space-y-2">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm text-red-500">3. Maxing Out the Spring Range</h3>
              <p class="text-mute leading-relaxed">Never operate a binding at its absolute maximum (e.g. DIN 11 on a 3–11 binding). Binding springs lose linearity near boundaries; upgrade to a higher-capacity binding.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-parchment border border-hairline space-y-2">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm text-red-500">4. Skipping the Mechanical Test</h3>
              <p class="text-mute leading-relaxed">A binding spring that hasn't been tested in 2 years may release 30% higher or lower than its visual indicator indicates due to dirt, dried grease, or spring fatigue.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* 6. CTA Box */}
        <div id="tech-card" class="p-8 rounded-3xl bg-primary/10 border border-primary/20 text-center space-y-4 scroll-mt-24">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink">6. Generate Your Verified Technician Card</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-mute max-w-lg mx-auto">
            Calculate your ISO 11088:2023 release setting and print our standardized Technician Card to bring directly to your certified ski shop.
          </p>
          <a
            href="/"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-full bg-primary text-canvas font-medium text-sm hover:scale-[0.98] transition-transform shadow-md"
          >
            Calculate & Print Tech Card
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Sticky Table of Contents (Desktop Sidebar) */}
      <aside class="hidden lg:block lg:col-span-4 lg:sticky lg:top-24 self-start">
        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-parchment border border-hairline space-y-4">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between border-b border-hairline pb-3">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink">Table of Contents</span>
            <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-primary animate-pulse"></span>
          </div>
          <nav class="space-y-1.5 text-xs font-mono" aria-label="Table of Contents">
            {tocItems.map(item => (
              <a
                href={`#${item.id}`}
                class="block px-3 py-2 rounded-lg text-mute hover:text-primary hover:bg-canvas transition-colors"
              >
                {item.title}
              </a>
            ))}
          </nav>
          <div class="pt-3 border-t border-hairline text-[11px] text-mute leading-relaxed">
            Jump to any section to review mechanical specs, forward pressure checks, or shop protocols.
          </div>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
